When to Use Different Sorting Algorithms:

Selection Sort

Best for small datasets or when memory swaps are expensive.
Has a consistent time complexity of O(n²), so it's not efficient for large datasets.
Useful when writing to memory (e.g., flash memory) is costly, as it makes at most O(n) swaps.

Insertion Sort
Best for small or nearly sorted datasets.
Has an average and worst-case time complexity of O(n²), but performs well on partially sorted arrays with O(n) complexity in the best case.
Used in hybrid sorting algorithms like Timsort and Introsort for small partitions.
Merge Sort

Best for large datasets and linked lists.
Works well with external sorting (e.g., sorting data on disk) due to its predictable O(n log n) complexity.
Preferred when stability is required (preserves the relative order of equal elements).
Quick Sort

Best for general-purpose sorting when an average O(n log n) complexity is desired.
Not stable, but usually faster than merge sort for in-memory sorting.
Used in standard libraries like C++ STL sort() and Python’s sorted().
Which Quick Sort Partitioning Scheme to Use:
Lomuto Partition (Single Pivot)

Simpler to implement but performs more swaps.
Performs worse when there are many duplicate elements.
Not ideal for large datasets due to extra swaps.
Hoare Partition (Dual Pivot)

More efficient as it reduces the number of swaps.
Works well for random and uniform distributions.
Better choice for quicksort in general-purpose sorting.
Median-of-Three Partitioning

Selects the median of three elements (first, middle, last) as a pivot.
Helps avoid worst-case O(n²) performance in already sorted or reverse-sorted arrays.
Used in optimized implementations of quicksort in real-world applications.
Best Partition Strategy:
If input is nearly sorted → Hoare partition with Median-of-Three.
If input has many duplicates → 3-way partitioning quicksort (Dutch National Flag algorithm).
If input is random → Hoare partition for best performance.